WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.190
Design well, check quality, price for profit.

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:09.750
This is the video for module three of Bead Making and Simple Crafts: design, quality and pricing.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:18.400
Two necklaces can use the same beads, yet one looks expensive and the other looks random. The difference is design.

4
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:25.860
Your customer judges your work twice: when she first sees it, and three weeks later after she has worn it.

5
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.580
You will learn to describe materials honestly.

6
00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:36.010
And you will learn a simple costing method, because many beaders lose money without knowing it.

7
00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:46.840
Use the colour wheel. One colour in light, medium and dark is calm and elegant. Two colours opposite each other, like royal blue with gold, are bold.

8
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:59.420
Start with about 60% of the beads in a main colour, 30% in a second colour and 10% in an accent. Equal amounts of two strong colours often fight each other.

9
00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:05.450
Give the necklace one focal point, a larger or special bead at the centre front.

10
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:14.910
Lay out the centre first, then build both sides at the same time so they mirror each other. Write the pattern down so you can make it again.

11
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:23.100
Colours change under a yellow bulb, so lay out your design near a window and take a phone photo before stringing.

12
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:33.350
First, the tug test. Hold the clasp in one hand and the beads near it in the other, and pull firmly. Repeat at the other end. Nothing should move.

13
00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:43.320
Second, the drape test. Hang the necklace by the clasp, then lay it on a rolled towel. It should curve smoothly with no stiff parts or gaps.

14
00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:59.060
Third, for every new design or material, wear one piece for at least a week. Ifeoma in Onitsha did this and found the gold coating rubbed off in 5 days. She stopped buying that bead before twenty customers were let down.

15
00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:08.290
Tick your checklist for every piece, not only big orders. In a WhatsApp world, one broken necklace can be shared with fifty people.

16
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.810
Do not call a piece gold, real coral, or say it will never break, unless you can prove it.

17
00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:30.560
Say gold-tone, coral-style, and give specific facts. Only say nickel-free or stainless steel when the supplier confirms it in writing. And never make bead jewellery for babies, because small beads are a choking hazard.

18
00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:38.600
Let us cost Kemi's necklace. These naira prices are approximate examples. Always use your own receipts.

19
00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:45.860
First, the unit cost. A pack of 100 crystals for about 2,000 naira is 20 naira per bead.

20
00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:53.850
66 crystals, spacers, wire and findings come to about 2,060 naira in materials.

21
00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.710
Labour is your time. She takes 1 hour at her chosen rate of 1,000 naira per hour.

22
00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.910
Packaging, a pouch and card, is about 250 naira.

23
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:12.830
Overheads like transport and data are 10% of materials, about 206 naira.

24
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:22.230
Her full cost is about 3,516 naira. Any price below that loses money, even if it feels like a sale.

25
00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:30.910
Profit is separate from your labour. Labour pays you for your time. Profit lets the business grow and survive slow months.

26
00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:43.700
Many small makers add 30 to 50% for retail. Kemi adds about 40%, about 1,406 naira, and rounds to 5,000 naira retail.

27
00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:53.590
Wholesale is for resellers. Kemi might set about 4,000 to 4,200 naira, with a minimum order of five pieces.

28
00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:01.200
Compare with similar pieces at your market and online, and update your costing sheet every time you restock.

29
00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:11.060
Selling below full cost means you pay to work. If a customer's budget is small, offer a simpler design with fewer or cheaper beads.

30
00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:29.170
To remember. Use 60-30-10 and a clear focal point. Tug, drape and wear test your work. Name materials honestly. Full cost is materials, labour, packaging and overheads. Add profit on top, and never sell below full cost.

31
00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:41.580
Now practise. Lay out three colour schemes, check three pieces against your own checklist, rewrite one product description honestly, and build a costing sheet for two pieces.

32
00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.870
Continue with the lessons below.
